            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Legalizing Christianity</strong><br>\nIn 313 AD, Constantine issued the Edict of Milan, which proclaimed religious tolerance for Christianity within the Roman Empire. Christians were no longer to be persecuted, and the confiscated property was to be returned to them. This edict was a monumental step for the Christian faith and allowed it to flourish within the empire, contributing to its spread throughout Europe.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Foundation of Constantinople</strong><br>\nConstantine understood the strategic importance of Byzantium and in 330 AD, he officially established the city as \"New Rome,\" later known as Constantinople. This city was to be a Christian city from its conception, adorned with grand churches and public buildings. Constantinople would later become the capital of the Eastern Roman Empire and a center for Christian scholarship and art.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Reforming the Empire</strong><br>\nConstantine enacted numerous administrative, social, and economic reforms to strengthen the empire. He restructured the government to provide more stability, reduced the military's influence in politics, and introduced new gold coins called solidus, which improved the economy. His reforms had a long-lasting impact, helping to extend the life of the Eastern Roman Empire for many centuries.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
