          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Relationship with the Church</strong><br>\nConstantine played a significant role in the early church, influencing the organization of Christianity and its doctrine. He convened the First Council of Nicaea in 325 AD, where the Nicene Creed was established, defining the fundamental beliefs of Christianity. His involvement signified the growing connection between the church and state, shaping the future of the empire and Christianity itself.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Constantine's Later Life</strong><br>\nThroughout his later years, Constantine continued to build churches and promote Christianity. He was the first Roman emperor to be baptized a Christian, which he did on his deathbed, reflecting his commitment to the faith he had endorsed and strengthened. His death in 337 AD marked the end of an era, but his legacy would continue to influence both political and religious spheres.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Constantine</strong><br>\nConstantine's reign is often seen as a turning point in history. He transformed the Roman Empire, setting the stage for the medieval period where Christianity was the dominant religion. The city he founded, Constantinople, stood for over a thousand years as the capital of the Byzantine Empire, until it fell to the Ottoman Turks in 1453. His influence is seen in the church, state affairs, military strategies, and economic policies of subsequent generations.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
